body:
### Version

1.64.0 and 1.64.1 (`webkit` r2370). Not in 1.63.0 (r2359) or `1.64.0-alpha-2026-10-01` (r2369). Fixed again in the 1.65 alphas: r2373, r2374, r2375 and r2376 all render normally.

### Steps to reproduce

`repro.js` measures `requestAnimationFrame` callbacks per second on a page with 12 translucent cards, with and without `backdrop-filter: blur(24px)`:

```js
const { webkit, devices } = require('playwright');

const html = (blur) => `<!doctype html><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width">
<style>
  body { margin: 0; background: #070a12 radial-gradient(1100px 620px at 12% -12%, rgba(79,139,255,.2), transparent 62%); }
  .card { margin: 16px; height: 120px; border-radius: 12px; background: rgba(13,17,27,.8); }
  .blur { backdrop-filter: blur(24px); -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(24px); }
</style>
${`<div class="card${blur ? ' blur' : ''}">card</div>`.repeat(12)}`;

// requestAnimationFrame callbacks per second, timed with setTimeout.
const fps = () =>
  new Promise((resolve) => {
    let frames = 0, done = false;
    const tick = () => { frames++; if (!done) requestAnimationFrame(tick); };
    requestAnimationFrame(tick);
    setTimeout(() => { done = true; resolve(Math.round(frames / 3)); }, 3000);
  });

(async () => {
  const browser = await webkit.launch();
  for (const blur of [false, true]) {
    const context = await browser.newContext({ ...devices['iPhone 13'] });
    const page = await context.newPage();
    await page.setContent(html(blur));
    console.log(`WebKit ${browser.version()}, 12 cards, backdrop-filter ${blur ? 'on ' : 'off'}: ${await page.evaluate(fps)} fps`);
    await context.close();
  }
  await browser.close();
})();
```

Run it in the official image on 2 CPUs. GitHub-hosted `ubuntu-latest` runners have 2 vCPUs:

```sh
docker run --rm --init --ipc=host --cpuset-cpus=0,1 -v "$PWD:/w:ro" mcr.microsoft.com/playwright:v1.64.1-noble \
  bash -c 'cd /tmp && npm i -s playwright@1.64.1 && npx playwright install webkit && cp /w/repro.js . && node repro.js'
```

To compare, replace `1.64.1` in the `npm i` with `1.63.0` or `1.65.0-alpha-2026-10-15`.

### Expected behavior

Frame rate close to the page without the filter, as on r2369 and earlier:

```
WebKit 26.6, 12 cards, backdrop-filter off: 53 fps
WebKit 26.6, 12 cards, backdrop-filter on : 48 fps
```

### Actual behavior

On r2370 the page gets no frames at all:

```
WebKit 27.2, 12 cards, backdrop-filter off: 53 fps
WebKit 27.2, 12 cards, backdrop-filter on : 0 fps
```

The 1.65.0-alpha-2026-10-15 build (r2376) gives 40 fps on the same command.

Fewer cards help: one card renders at ~40–50 fps on r2370. So does a lower `deviceScaleFactor`: at 1, the 12 cards render at 22 fps (arm64) to 43 fps (x86_64) on 2 CPUs. With 18 CPUs, r2370 renders the 12 cards at ~28 fps, half the rate of the page without the filter. So r2370 seems to spend far more CPU on each backdrop-filter layer, and 2 CPUs cannot keep up. Adding `box-shadow` to the cards makes no difference. Neither do `WEBKIT_SKIA_ENABLE_CPU_RENDERING=1`, `WEBKIT_DISABLE_DMABUF_RENDERER=1`, `LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1`, `reducedMotion: 'reduce'` or video recording.

In a test suite it does not look like a rendering problem:
- every click waits seconds for "visible, enabled and stable";
- CSS animations never reach `animationend`, so a Radix dialog stays mounted with `data-state="closed"` and `toBeHidden()` times out;
- because the closed dialog stays mounted, the `aria-hidden` it puts on the rest of the page stays too, and `getByRole` stops finding anything outside it.

One shard of our mobile-safari (iPhone 13) project on `ubuntu-latest` went from 10 minutes to hitting its 30-minute job timeout.

### Additional context

Measured on GitHub-hosted `ubuntu-latest` (2 vCPU, x86_64; Intel Xeon Platinum 8573C and AMD EPYC 9V74), and in Docker on arm64 pinned to 2 CPUs, with the same result on both. r2370 is the roll from #43084 that took the WebKit base forward for #42940, so the regression would be in WebKit between the r2369 and r2370 bases, and gone again by r2373 (#43191).

Since r2373 and later are fine, is a 1.64.x patch with a newer WebKit planned? If not, this at least records that 1.64.0 and 1.64.1 are affected, for anyone whose Linux WebKit suite slowed to timeouts after upgrading. We are holding at 1.63 until 1.65.

### Environment

```yaml
System:
  OS: Ubuntu 24.04 (mcr.microsoft.com/playwright:v1.64.1-noble)
  CPU: 2 vCPU (GitHub-hosted ubuntu-latest, x86_64); also arm64 Docker with --cpuset-cpus=0,1
  GPU: none (Mesa llvmpipe)
Binaries:
  Node: 22.23.3 and the image's own Node
npmPackages:
  playwright: 1.64.1
```
